.h1 About this document [errata-about]
This document lists the anomalies that are known in the terminal programs, the
Editor and the Document Manager when they run in a terminal.  An anomaly is
something that looks wrong and is not a mistake of yours.  Each entry says what
is seen, what is known about the cause and what is not, and what to do.  Where
a cause is only suspected, the entry says so and gives a test that tells.

The list is short, and it grows.  The window version of the Editor is not in
it, except where an entry says so.  The faults and limits that the project has
decided not to fix, such as those of the Printer, are in the file
{c:KNOWN-ISSUES.txt} that is installed with the documents.

.h1 The entries [errata-entries]
The entries are in the order in which they were found.  An entry is a
section of its own, so that another document can refer to it.

.h2 PgUp and PgDn of the main block do nothing in xterm [errata-pgup]
{b:What is seen.}  The keys {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} between the keyboard and the
keypad do nothing in the terminal programs run in an {c:xterm}.  The {k:PgUp}
and {k:PgDn} of the keypad work, and so does {k:Ins}.

{b:What is known.}  The keys never reach the program, so the program is not the
cause.  On the desktop where this was seen, {c:xev} shows both keys with no
modifier: the {k:PgDn} of the main block as keycode 117, keysym {c:Next}, and
the {k:PgDn} of the keypad as keycode 89, keysym {c:KP_Next}.  So the keyboard
is right.  The command {c:cat -v}, run in that {c:xterm}, prints nothing for
the main {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} and prints {c:^[[5~} and {c:^[[6~} for the keypad
ones.  In a plain {c:xterm} 407 on a clean machine both keys send the same two
codes, and the terminal programs treat the codes alike, so the two keys behave
the same there; the window version treats the two key names alike too.

{b:What is not known.}  Why the keys are lost on that desktop.  The session
runs the input method IBus with its X input method switched on
({c:ibus-daemon --xim}, with {c:XMODIFIERS} set to {c:@im=ibus}), under
WindowMaker.  IBus uses the Page Up and Page Down keys for its lists of
candidates, so the input method is the suspect.  It is not proven.  The
suspicion could not be reproduced on a clean machine with IBus running and its
default engines: both keys arrived there.

{b:What to do.}  To find out whether the input method is the cause, start an
{c:xterm} with no input method and look at the keys again:
.code
XMODIFIERS= xterm
cat -v
.end
Press the main {k:PgDn} and then the keypad {k:PgDn}.  If both print
{c:^[[6~}, the input method is the cause: start {c:xterm} that way, or switch
the X input method of IBus off for it.  If the main key still prints nothing,
the cause is elsewhere (a binding of the terminal or of the window manager).
Meanwhile use the keypad {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} (with NumLock off), or the
arrow keys.

.h2 Keys that a terminal or a desktop keeps for itself [errata-keys]
{b:What is seen.}  A key does nothing, or does what the terminal does, when it
is pressed in a terminal program.  Examples are {k:Shift-Ins}, {k:Shift-PgUp}
and {k:Shift-PgDn}, the {k:Alt} keys, the keypad {k:+}, {k:-} and {k:*}, and
{k:Ctrl-I}, which is {k:Tab}.

{b:What is known.}  The terminal takes some keys before they reach the program,
sends the same code for two keys, or sends a plain character in place of a
command.  {c:xterm}, {c:tmux} and {c:xfce4-terminal} were probed and the table
of their keys is in {x:appterm-reach} and in {x:view-terminal-keys}, with a
key to use in place of each.

{b:What to do.}  Use the key that the table gives.  The alternates work in the
window version too.

.h2 Lines and shapes drawn as odd characters [errata-chars]
{b:What is seen.}  The boxes of the screen are drawn as letters with accents,
as squares or as empty squares.

{b:What is known.}  The screen of a terminal program has three forms, and the
UTF-8 form draws the double lines and the shapes of the character set.  It is
right only in a terminal that is in UTF-8 and has a font with the shapes.  In
a terminal that is not in UTF-8 each shape shows as several odd
letters; where the font lacks the shape a square is drawn.  Both were seen in
{c:xterm} 407, the squares with the bitmap font {c:fixed} in a UTF-8 locale.
For that reason the screen is the VT100 form unless you choose another: it
draws single lines with the VT100 line characters, which nearly every terminal
has, and needs neither UTF-8 nor a special font.  The VT100 form has no double
lines, so a double line is drawn single.

{b:What to do.}  Use the field Terminal Characters of Setup, on its third page,
to choose Automatic, ASCII, VT100 or UTF-8.  {x:term-choose} says how it works
and {x:term-check} gives two commands that show what your terminal does.

.h2 A letter with an accent is shown as another letter [errata-accents]
{b:What is seen.}  On the VT100 screen and on the ASCII screen a letter with an
accent, a Greek letter or a shape of the character set shows as the plain
letter that looks most like it, or as a question mark.

{b:What is known.}  The two screens write ASCII characters only.  The text of
the document is not changed: the document keeps the letter, also when it is
saved, and the letter is shown right on the UTF-8 screen and in the window
version.

{b:What to do.}  To see the letters, set Terminal Characters to UTF-8 in a
terminal that is in UTF-8 and whose font has them (see
{x:errata-chars}), or use the window version.

.h2 Terminals that report keys with several modifiers [errata-modified]
{b:What is seen.}  Nothing is known to be wrong.  This entry states a limit of
the checks.

{b:What is known.}  Some terminals send a distinct code for a key held with
Ctrl, Alt and Shift together, for example the Ctrl-Shift forms of the arrow
keys.  The terminal programs read these codes, but no terminal of that kind
was run: the reading was checked against the documented codes.

{b:What to do.}  If such a key does not act as the key tables say, use the
alternate in {x:appterm-reach} and report what the terminal sends, which
{c:cat -v} shows.
